    "## 1. Why a passing network is a modeling choice\n",
    "\n",
    "It's not trivial to create a passing network, and doing so is an explicit modeling choice. We only get a graph after we decide what the nodes are, what counts as an edge, and which events we want to aggregate. In the main representation used here:\n",
    "\n",
    "- nodes = players,\n",
    "- directed edge $i \\to j$ = player $i$ passed to player $j$,\n",
    "- edge weight $w_{ij}$ = the number of qualifying passes from $i$ to $j$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Even that apparently simple definition depends on choices. \n",
    "- We must decide which team to isolate,\n",
    "- which time window to use,\n",
    "- whether to count completed passes or all attempted passes with a known intended recipient,\n",
    "- whether to keep only starters or all players who appear in a phase,\n",
    "- whether to exclude set pieces, and\n",
    "- whether to preserve pitch geometry\n",
    "- and more??"
   ]
  },
