    "### 7.1 Strength: how much passing volume a player sends and receives\n",
    "\n",
    "Let $w_{ij}$ be the number of passes from player $i$ to player $j$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Then the **out-strength** of player $i$ is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "s_i^{out} = \\sum_j w_{ij},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and the **in-strength** is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "s_i^{in} = \\sum_j w_{ji}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "These are weighted versions of degree. In soccer language:\n",
    "\n",
    "- large $s_i^{out}$ means a player **distributed** many qualifying passes,\n",
    "- large $s_i^{in}$ means a player **received** many qualifying passes.\n",
    "\n",
    "In code, these come from `G.out_degree(weight=\"weight\")` and `G.in_degree(weight=\"weight\")`. They are often the clearest first description of circulation volume in a passing network (Duch, Waitzman, and Amaral 2010; Buldú et al. 2018).\n"
   ]
  },
