    "### 7.2 Degree centrality: how many different teammates a player connects with\n",
    "\n",
    "Strength counts **volume**. Degree centrality counts **breadth**. If $N$ is the number of nodes in the graph and $k_i^{out}$ is the number of distinct outgoing neighbors of $i$, then\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C_{D}^{out}(i) = \\frac{k_i^{out}}{N - 1}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Similarly, if $k_i^{in}$ is the number of distinct incoming neighbors,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C_{D}^{in}(i) = \\frac{k_i^{in}}{N - 1}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "In soccer language:\n",
    "\n",
    "- high **out-degree centrality** means a player distributed to many different teammates,\n",
    "- high **in-degree centrality** means a player was found by many different teammates.\n",
    "\n",
    "These are computed by `nx.out_degree_centrality(G)` and `nx.in_degree_centrality(G)` (NetworkX Developers 2025a, 2025b). A player can have high strength but only moderate degree centrality if most of their passing is concentrated in a few channels.\n"
   ]
  },
