    "### 7.3 Betweenness centrality: who lies on the strongest short routes?\n",
    "\n",
    "Betweenness is a brokerage measure. For player $v$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C_B(v) = \\sum_{s \\neq v \\neq t,\\ s \\neq t} \\frac{\\sigma_{st}(v)}{\\sigma_{st}},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where:\n",
    "\n",
    "- $s$ and $t$ are source and target nodes,\n",
    "- $\\sigma_{st}$ is the number of shortest paths from $s$ to $t$,\n",
    "- $\\sigma_{st}(v)$ is the number of those shortest paths that pass through $v$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because our edge weights are pass counts, we do **not** use the raw weight as a distance. We instead define\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "d_{ij} = \\frac{1}{w_{ij}}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "That makes heavily used links behave like **shorter** or **easier** routes through the network. In code, that is why our graph stores both `weight` and `distance` on each edge. Betweenness then comes from `nx.betweenness_centrality(G, weight=\"distance\")` (Freeman 1977; NetworkX Developers 2025c)."
   ]
  },
