    "### 7.4 Harmonic centrality: how easily can the rest of the network reach a player through short strong chains?\n",
    "\n",
    "On a directed graph, ordinary closeness can behave awkwardly when some nodes are not mutually reachable. A more stable option is **harmonic centrality**. In the form implemented by `networkx`,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C_H(u) = \\sum_{v \\neq u} \\frac{1}{d(v, u)},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $d(v, u)$ is the shortest-path distance from $v$ to $u$, and unreachable nodes contribute $0$ (Marchiori and Latora 2000; NetworkX Developers 2025d).\n",
    "\n",
    "Because our edge distances are `distance = 1 / weight`, large pass counts shrink the path cost. In this directed setting, the default `networkx` convention is an **incoming-reach** notion: a player scores highly when many teammates can reach them through short strong paths.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Caveat.** If you wanted an outward-reach version instead, you would reverse the graph first. Direction matters.\n"
   ]
  },
