    "## 9. Interpreting the real first-half networks\n",
    "\n",
    "A careful comparison needs to keep the match story and the graph story in conversation. Argentina's first-half network is denser and more reciprocal than France's under the same starter-only filter. Its strongest reciprocal links run through the spine of the team, especially Otamendi, Romero, Enzo Fernández, and Rodrigo De Paul. The adjacency matrix is not uniformly filled, but it does show a broad backbone rather than just one or two isolated corridors.\n",
    "\n",
    "France's first-half network looks thinner and more concentrated in the back line. Raphaël Varane and Upamecano dominate several node-level measures, and Jules Koundé also carries substantial brokerage. That does **not** mean those defenders were France's best attacking players. It means the team's first-half circulation under this filter often ran through deeper stabilization points before meaningful progression could happen. The early substitutions matter here, because the first-half French structure was not stable for the full 45 minutes.\n",
    "\n",
    "The average-$x$ versus strength view adds one more layer. Argentina get large total-strength contributions from both deeper and more advanced nodes, whereas France's largest totals sit deeper. That fits the first-half match narrative without overclaiming. A passing network can support a tactical reading, but it should not pretend to be a full tactical model (Grund 2012; Ribeiro et al. 2017; Buldú et al. 2018)."
   ]
  },
