    "## 12. Closing synthesis\n",
    "\n",
    "By this point, the main ideas should be clear. A passing network starts with event data. **We** choose nodes, edges, filters, and a time window, etc.\n",
    "\n",
    "We built one baseline network fully in the open: pass rows $to$ edge list $to$ adjacency matrix $to$ directed weighted graph. We then looked at that same graph through several node-level properties, including strength, degree centrality, betweenness, harmonic centrality, PageRank, and HITS.\n",
    "\n",
    "The World Cup Final also makes the limitations of static networks very concrete. First-half networks, second-half networks, score-state summaries, and extra-time networks do not tell the same story. That is not a bug. It is the point. Passing networks are most useful when we treat them as carefully defined summaries of interaction structure, not as self-evident truths about a match.\n",
    "\n",
    "What do passing networks capture well? They capture who interacted with whom, how heavily, how reciprocally, and with what structural asymmetries. What do they miss? They miss defensive pressure, off-ball movement, available-but-unchosen options, the value of carries, and much of the tactical context that lives outside the pass itself. Good analysis keeps both sides in view."
   ]
  },
